.Dd Jun 29, 2024
.Dt LOGREAD 1
.Os sysklogd
.Sh NAME
.Nm logread
.Nd Read the in-memory log buffer from syslogd
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl fhv
.Op Fl e Ar PATTERN
.Op Fl u Ar PATH
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints the contents of the in-memory log buffer maintained by
.Xr syslogd 8 ,
in the style of the BusyBox and OpenWRT tool of the same name.  It is a
lightweight way to inspect recent log activity on systems where logs are
kept in RAM rather than written to flash.
.Pp
The buffer must be enabled in
.Xr syslog.conf 5
with the
.Ql membuf
directive, which also defines the control socket
.Nm
connects to and who may read it.  Without it,
.Nm
has nothing to connect to.
.Pp
By default
.Nm
prints the buffered messages and exits.  With
.Fl f
it stays connected and prints new messages as they arrive, like
.Ql tail -f .
.Sh OPTIONS
This program follows the usual UNIX command line syntax:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl e Ar PATTERN
Only print lines containing
.Ar PATTERN .
.It Fl f
Follow mode.  After printing the buffered messages, keep the connection
open and print new messages as they are logged.
.It Fl h
Show the built-in help text.
.It Fl u Ar PATH
Connect to the control socket at
.Ar PATH
instead of the default.  Must match the path given to
.Ql membuf
in
.Xr syslog.conf 5 .
.It Fl v
Show program version.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -unfilled -offset left
logread
logread -f
logread -e dropbear
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr logger 1
.Xr syslog.conf 5
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.Nm
was written by Joachim Wiberg for the sysklogd project to complement
.Xr syslogd 8 .
